I've recently turned my patching-fu to the Floating Eye Problem in
nethack.  There are two existing patches on bilious, but neither seemed
satisfactory to me.

Really, the problem isn't actually specific to floating eyes.  No one
ever forgets that meleeing blue "e"s is a bad idea -- the problem is
that in the early game where you have no light, *any* monster can appear
in your way with a single turn's warning.  Floating eyes are just the
first problematic monster for a non-pacifist, non-protection-racket
player.

The "least cheaty" (for those who think slowing down and relying on the
travel command is the only true course) of the existing patches is
"justmove".  It turns every move into an "m"-prefixed move.  But that
means if you accidentally bump a monster, you waste a turn in front of
it.  Which can be bad for things other than eyes.

The other one, "Floating eye attack confirmation", is only good for
floating eyes, and also goes above what is necessary in cheatiness,
as by not prompting it will tell you if you have reflection or FA.

Now, my solution, "bumplock".  Under the bumplock patch, you can type
"m." to lock your melee attack.  If you bump into a monster (other than
a pet with safepet on), the bumplock will trip, and all further commands
will be refused until you acknowledge it by typing backspace.

By locking the command input, it will save you from more than one
keypress rendered obsolete by an unexpected encounter.

The bumplock turns off whenever you use the "F" prefix.  Pressing
backspace resets it but does not disarm it -- you must use "F" to ever
melee again.

It's available from:

ftp://talamasca.ocis.net/pub/michael/beta/nethack-3.4.3-bumplock-1.diff

This is a young patch, invented less than a week ago, so I'm calling it
beta for now.

Note that I tried to make the README.bumplock in the patch itself
non-spoily, so it says nothing explicit about eyes...

